Creating a phishing simulation program for educational or research purposes should be done carefully to ensure it doesn't cause harm or violate ethical guidelines. Below is an example of a simple, non-malicious phishing simulation that you can use to study user responses. This simulation is designed to look like a legitimate email but is clearly marked as a test.

### Components of the Phishing Simulation

1. **Email Template**: A phishing email typically includes:
   - A sense of urgency
   - A familiar brand
   - A clear call-to-action (e.g., "Click here to login")
   - A placeholder for a malicious link or action

2. **Fake Login Page**: After clicking the link, the user is taken to a fake login page that looks like a real one.

3. **Data Collection**: Track which users fall for the phishing attempt and how they respond.

Here's an example implementation:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Phishing Simulation</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            line-height: 1.6;
        }
        .email-container {
            max-width: 600px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            padding: 20px;
        }
        .logo {
            height: 80px;
            width: 200px;
        }
        .button {
            display: inline-block;
            background-color: #007bff;
            color: white;
            padding: 10px 20px;
            text-decoration: none;
            border-radius: 4px;
            margin-top: 20px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="email-container">
        <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/200x80" alt="Company Logo" class="logo">
        <h1>Security Alert: Your Account Access Required</h1>
        <p>Dear Valued Customer,</p>
        <p>Your account security is important to us. We have noticed unusual activity on your account.</p>
        <p>To ensure your account remains secure, please click the button below to verify your account:</p>
        <center>
            <a href="https://localhost:8080/login.php" class="button">Verify Now</a>
        </center>
        <p>If you did not request this email, please ignore it. This is a test of our security systems.</p>
        <p>Best regards,<br>
        The Support Team</p>
    </div>

    <script>
        // Track which button is clicked
        document.querySelectorAll('a').forEach(button => {
            button.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
                const url = e.target.href;
                const buttonText = e.target.textContent;

                // Collect data here (e.g., send to your server)
                fetch('https://your-server.com/track.php', {
                    method: 'POST',
                    headers: {
                        'Content-Type': 'application/json',
                    },
                    body: JSON.stringify({
                        email: 'test@example.com',  // Replace with actual email
                        clicked_link: url,
                        clicked_button: buttonText
                    })
                });
            });
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Steps to Use the Simulation:

1. **Set Up the Domain**: Replace `your-server.com` with your own test domain or use `localhost` if you're testing locally.

2. **Add Your Branding**: Replace the placeholder logo with your company's logo or use a placeholder image.

3. **Modify the Message**: Tailor the email content to match your organization's branding and the purpose of your test.

4. **Run the Simulation**: Send the email to your test group and observe their responses.

5. **Analyze Results**: Collect data on which users were phished and how they responded.

### Important Notes:

- **Use a Test Domain**: Ensure you own or control the domain you're using for the simulation to avoid confusion.
- **Use Localhost or a Safe Server**: Run the simulation on a secure, internal server or localhost.
- **Educational Use**: Use this simulation for educational purposes only to help users recognize phishing attempts.
- **Data Privacy**: Ensure you handle user data responsibly and delete test data after the simulation.

### Ethical Considerations:

- Always inform participants that they are part of a simulation.
- Avoid using real user information or sensitive data.
- Ensure the simulation complies with local laws and regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA).

This simulation can be further customized to match specific types of phishing attacks, such as password reset requests, account verification, or payment requests. The key is to make it realistic enough to study user behavior while ensuring it doesn't cause harm.<｜end▁of▁sentence｜>

